gaslight
========

Flaring analysis for the Permian Basin. Gaslight cross-references
satellite-detected flares against Texas state records — permits, wells, leases,
reported flaring volumes, and methane plumes — to show where gas is being burned
and how that compares with what operators report to regulators.

Live map: http://research.datadesk.eco/gaslight/

the data
--------

The dataset is a single, standalone DuckDB file, `dist/gaslight.duckdb` — one
plain schema, latitude/longitude columns, no geometry blobs. Open it with the
duckdb CLI, or read it straight into pandas, R, or QGIS:

    duckdb dist/gaslight.duckdb

Every table and column is documented in `docs/data-dictionary/`. The same
documentation is embedded in the database itself (`SELECT * FROM _dictionary`).

Note that the flaring volumes are self-reported by operators to the RRC, while
the satellite layers are independent observations from space; the two do not
always agree. The Sentinel-2 flare layer is detected by the sibling
permian-flaring project — gaslight fetches and displays it (`make s2`).

building it
-----------

    uv sync
    make refresh     # rebuild the database from scratch
    make serve       # run the map locally on :8080
    make help        # all targets

The scrapers and SQL pipeline live in `scripts/` and `queries/`; the web map is
in `web/` (MapLibre GL + DuckDB WASM, no build step, no npm dependencies). Push
to main to deploy via GitHub Pages.
